What `git init` writes into `.git/hooks`, and the other files that collect
there. git runs a hook by the name it looks for, so none of these is one, and
a wildcard would call every repository ever initialised a dozen surfaces.

The sample content is git's own, which carries nothing any rule flags: the
cost of matching these would be noise in a count rather than false findings,
and a count is what a prevalence figure is.

>>> .git/hooks/pre-commit.sample
#!/bin/sh
exec git diff-index --check --cached HEAD --
>>> .git/hooks/commit-msg.sample
#!/bin/sh
test "" = "$(grep '^Signed-off-by: ' "$1" | sort | uniq -c | sed -n '/^[ ]*1[ ]/p')"
>>> .git/hooks/README
Hooks live here. The samples above are shipped by git init.
